# ohmail self-host configuration.
#
#   cp .env.example .env        # then fill in the five required values below
#   docker compose up -d --build
#   docker compose logs api     # the first-run setup token is printed here, once
#
# Everything the stack reads is in this file. Values marked REQUIRED fail
# `docker compose config` with a message naming the variable; everything else has a
# working default baked into docker-compose.yml and only needs touching when the
# sentence beside it applies to your install.

# ── Required ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# The ONE browser origin this install serves — scheme + host, nothing else.
#   https://mail.example.com   a real domain: Caddy provisions TLS automatically, provided
#                              the domain resolves to this box and ports 80/443 are reachable.
#   http://localhost           a same-box run, plain HTTP on loopback. Works in every major
#                              browser except Safari, which refuses the product's Secure
#                              cookies over http://localhost — use the HTTPS form there.
#   https://ohmail.test        a PRIVATE name on your own network (.test, .lan, .internal, or
#                              any name only your DNS knows). Set OHMAIL_TLS_INTERNAL=1 below
#                              — without it Caddy asks Let's Encrypt for a certificate it can
#                              never issue, and retries for thirty days while your site serves
#                              no TLS at all.
# This value derives the cookie host and the passkey identity; changing it later signs
# everybody out.
OHMAIL_ORIGIN=

# Set to 1 when OHMAIL_ORIGIN is a PRIVATE name — anything a public certificate authority
# cannot validate from the internet. Caddy then issues certificates from its own local CA
# instead of going to ACME. Leave it empty for a real public domain and for http:// origins.
#
# Browsers will not trust that CA until you install its root, which the stack holds here:
#   docker compose exec proxy cat /data/caddy/pki/authorities/local/root.crt > ohmail-local-ca.crt
# Install that file as a trusted root on every machine that opens the app (on Arch:
# `sudo trust anchor ohmail-local-ca.crt`; on Debian/Ubuntu, copy to
# /usr/local/share/ca-certificates/ and run `sudo update-ca-certificates`).
#
# Caddy picks its local CA automatically for `localhost` and for bare IP addresses, so those
# two origins need nothing here.
OHMAIL_TLS_INTERNAL=

# The database password (user and database are both `ohmail`, created on first start).
# URL-safe characters only — the value is embedded in a connection URL.
POSTGRES_PASSWORD=

# The key that encrypts mailbox credentials at rest. 64 hex characters:
#   openssl rand -hex 32
# Losing it means every connected mailbox has to be re-entered; treat it like a backup key.
# To ROTATE, add TF_KEK_V2 (then TF_KEK_V3) with a fresh value and KEEP the older versions
# set — new writes use the highest version, old rows still decrypt with theirs. Never replace
# TF_KEK_V1 in place: rows encrypted under it would become unreadable.
TF_KEK_V1=
#TF_KEK_V2=
#TF_KEK_V3=

# The credential for the bundled attachment-staging store (MinIO). Never leaves the
# compose network, but pick a real value:
#   openssl rand -hex 24
MINIO_ROOT_PASSWORD=

# This install's ORGANIZER IDENTITY — the name its claim carries in each connected
# mailbox's `ohmail/_meta` folder, which is how every ohmail deployment (this one, the
# hosted service, a desktop install) agrees on WHO organizes a mailbox. Generate once:
#   echo "ohmail-selfhost:$(openssl rand -hex 6)"
# and never change it afterwards: a changed value makes this install read its own
# previous claims as a stranger's, and every mailbox then waits for you to press
# "Organize here" again. It must be UNIQUE to this install. Without a value the
# organizer falls back to the same identity the hosted service runs under, and two
# organizers sharing one name cannot tell each other's claims from their own — the
# exact dual-organizing the claim exists to prevent, reached the moment a mailbox has
# ever been organized by ohmail Cloud or by a second install.
TF_ORGANIZER_INSTALL_ID=

# What this server calls ITSELF on other people's screens. The identity above is an opaque
# handle; this is the string a person reads on their desktop app when it says who organizes
# their mailbox, and the one written into `ohmail/_meta` for any mail client to see.
#
# LEAVE IT EMPTY unless you want something other than your own address there: empty means the
# organizer derives the name from OHMAIL_ORIGIN's host, so `https://mail.example.com` reads as
# "Organized by mail.example.com", which is true and is what an operator's own users expect.
# Set it only to say something the origin does not:
#   TF_ORGANIZER_DISPLAY_NAME=Front office server
TF_ORGANIZER_DISPLAY_NAME=

# ── Outbound mail ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# Where product mail (address verification, new-device notices) is submitted. The default
# is the bundled mailpit SINK: mail is viewable at http://127.0.0.1:8025 on the box and
# delivered nowhere. Point this at a real relay to deliver for real:
#   SMTP_URL=smtps://user:password@smtp.example.com:465
# SMTP_URL and MAIL_FROM travel together — setting one without the other refuses the boot.
#SMTP_URL=smtp://mailpit:1025
#MAIL_FROM=ohmail@selfhost.example
#MAIL_REPLY_TO=

# ── Optional ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# Pin the ohmail images to a released version instead of `latest`, e.g.
#   OHMAIL_IMAGE_TAG=0.9.8
#OHMAIL_IMAGE_TAG=

# AI features (screening suggestions, reply drafting). Absent means those surfaces answer
# as unconfigured — everything else works. You supply the key, you pay the model bill.
#
# THIS STACK TAKES AN ANTHROPIC KEY AND NOTHING ELSE. The desktop app also offers an OpenAI
# key and a local model over Ollama; this server composition does not wire either yet, and
# an OPENAI_API_KEY set here is read by nothing. docs/self-host/README.md has the table.
#ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=

# Set to exactly `1` when your mail server lives on your own LAN (a private IP or an
# internal hostname). The add-mailbox connection probe refuses private-network targets by
# default, because on a multi-user server it dials whatever address a signed-in user typed.
#TF_PROBE_ALLOW_PRIVATE=

# Set to exactly `1` when your UnifiedPush DISTRIBUTOR lives on your own LAN (an `ntfy`
# beside this server). The mobile app can register an endpoint so your server wakes the
# phone when mail arrives — a signal with no subject, no sender and no count in it. By
# default that endpoint must be `https` and must resolve to a public address, because the
# organizer POSTs to it unattended for as long as the registration lives.
#
# A SEPARATE switch from TF_PROBE_ALLOW_PRIVATE on purpose. The two answer different
# questions: "may an interactive probe dial my LAN mail server once, behind a step-up
# session" and "may a background process POST to my LAN for as long as a registration
# lives". Saying yes to the first is not saying yes to the second.
#
# It has to be set on BOTH services or neither — the api validates the registration and
# the organizer dials it, and a value on only one of them is a phone with a registration
# nothing ever rings.
#TF_PUSH_ALLOW_PRIVATE=

# ── Encrypted new-mail wakes: this install's own VAPID keypair ────────────────────────────
#
# The mobile app registers with your phone's UnifiedPush distributor by handing it a PUBLIC
# key, and from then on it renders only wakes signed with the matching PRIVATE key. So a wake
# a phone will actually act on needs a keypair, and it has to be YOURS — never copied from
# another install, ours included. Two installs sharing a private key means either can send
# wakes the other's phones accept, and neither operator can tell.
#
# Generate one on this machine, nothing is sent anywhere:
#
#     node scripts/vapid-keygen.mjs
#
# and paste the two lines it prints below. `node scripts/vapid-keygen.mjs --check` verifies
# what is set — including whether the two halves are actually a pair, which is the mistake a
# copy-paste makes and the one nothing else will ever report: phones register happily and
# never ring.
#
# The public key goes to BOTH services (the api serves it to phones, the organizer puts it in
# every wake); the private key goes to the organizer alone, because the api never signs.
# Leave both empty and encrypted wakes are simply off — mail still arrives, the app still
# syncs when you open it, and a raw consumer (an `ntfy` topic you watch yourself) still gets
# the plain fifteen-byte wake. Set them WRONG and the organizer refuses to send any wake at
# all, deliberately, rather than let the arm that still works hide a broken configuration.
#
# Rotating the pair costs one re-registration per phone and nothing else.
#TF_VAPID_PUBLIC_KEY=
#TF_VAPID_PRIVATE_KEY=
# Optional: a mailto: or https: URI a push service can use to contact you about a misbehaving
# sender (RFC 8292's `sub`). Omitted from the signed token when empty, which is conformant.
#TF_VAPID_SUBJECT=

# Operational alerting: a shared secret (24+ characters) arms the alert intake, and the
# webhook URL is where alerts are POSTed as JSON (ntfy.sh, Slack/Discord hooks, PagerDuty).
# Absent means the alert endpoints answer 404 and the organizer only logs.
#TF_ALERT_SECRET=
#TF_ALERT_WEBHOOK_URL=

# Microsoft 365 / Exchange Online mailboxes need an app registration; password/IMAP
# mailboxes need none of this. It matters more than it used to: Microsoft has been
# switching basic authentication off, and on a tenant where that has already happened an
# app password will not connect at all.
#
# THERE ARE TWO WAYS IN. Set either block, or neither. If you set both, the app uses your
# own registration — it is yours, and it does not put your users behind a client id that
# other installs share.

# ── OPTION 1: BRING YOUR OWN REGISTRATION (a confidential app, with a secret) ──
#
# Register your own application in Entra, then set the four values below. Register
#   <OHMAIL_ORIGIN>/api/mailboxes/oauth/microsoft/callback
# as the redirect URI — the stack derives exactly that value by default, so only set
# MS_OAUTH_REDIRECT_URI if your registration deliberately uses a different one.
# Delegated permissions: IMAP.AccessAsUser.All, SMTP.Send, openid, email (offline_access
# is requested automatically — without it a mailbox stops syncing after an hour).
#MS_OAUTH_CLIENT_ID=
#MS_OAUTH_CLIENT_SECRET=
#MS_OAUTH_TENANT=
#MS_OAUTH_REDIRECT_URI=

# ── OPTION 2: THE DEVICE-CODE FLOW (a public app, no secret, no redirect URI) ──
#
# Connecting a mailbox this way shows the person a short code and a URL; they enter it at
# microsoft.com on any device, approve the sign-in, and Microsoft issues the access
# DIRECTLY TO THIS SERVER. Nothing about the exchange passes through ohmail's
# infrastructure, and there is no redirect URI — which is exactly why it works on a server
# whose hostname Microsoft has never heard of.
#
# MS_DEVICE_CLIENT_ID is the Application (client) ID of a PUBLIC application registration.
# It is a DIFFERENT registration from option 1 and the ids are not interchangeable: a
# confidential app's id fails this flow outright, because the flow sends no secret and
# Entra answers `unauthorized_client`. So this is its own variable and the app reads only
# this one for this door.
#
# You can point it at your own public registration today. If you do, in Entra:
#   · Platform "Mobile and desktop applications", and
#   · Authentication -> Advanced settings -> "Allow public client flows" = Yes. Without
#     this the flow returns `unauthorized_client` and nothing works. It is the single
#     easiest step to miss.
#   · Same delegated permissions as option 1. NO client secret — do not create one; this
#     door refuses to send one deliberately.
#
# ohmail's own shared public client id will be published here once it has been registered
# and tested against real Microsoft. Until that line exists, this variable works with a
# registration you make yourself.
#
# MS_DEVICE_TENANT is optional and defaults to `common`, which is what lets both a work
# mailbox and an outlook.com mailbox sign in. Set it only for a single-tenant registration.
#
# NOTE: set this on BOTH the `api` and the `organizer` services. The api runs the sign-in;
# the organizer renews the access afterwards, for ever, and a refresh token can only be
# renewed by the application that issued it. Armed on the api alone, a mailbox connects and
# then stops syncing about an hour later.
#MS_DEVICE_CLIENT_ID=
#MS_DEVICE_TENANT=

# ── Storage details (defaults are the bundled MinIO; change only for an external store) ──

# The staging store's endpoint, region, access key and bucket. To use an existing
# S3-compatible service instead of the bundled MinIO, set all of these to that service's
# values — and remember the organizer sweeps the same bucket the api mints into.
#S3_ENDPOINT=http://minio:9000
#S3_REGION=us-east-1
#MINIO_ROOT_USER=ohmail-staging
#S3_BUCKET=ohmail-staging

# Where a BROWSER uploads staged attachments. Leave this unset: uploads then go to
# <OHMAIL_ORIGIN>/<bucket>/…, and the proxy carries them to MinIO — nothing extra to expose.
# A different value only serves NON-BROWSER clients (the desktop app): the web app may only
# talk to its own origin, so an off-origin endpoint here means browser uploads are refused
# by the browser itself. If S3_ENDPOINT names an external store and you still want browser
# uploads, route /<bucket>/* to that store in the Caddyfile instead — it must accept requests
# addressed under this origin (your own MinIO does; Amazon's does not).
#S3_PUBLIC_ENDPOINT=
